\section*{Thomas–Fermi asymptotics for growing atomic ionization number}

\subsection{OpenAI's claim}
OpenAI presents a proof in the selected manuscript. Removing $m$ electrons from a full Coulomb atom is claimed to cost asymptotically $a_{\mathrm{TF}}m^{7/3}$ when $m\to\infty$ and $Z/m\to\infty$.

\subsection{Outline of the argument}
Positive-price particle sectors and an exterior cutoff isolate the outer electrons. Conditional screening comparison continues a lower barrier, identifies the limiting field and charge, and then returns to fixed particle sectors to recover the Thomas--Fermi coefficient.

\subsection{Scope relative to this problem}
Removing $m$ electrons from a full Coulomb atom is claimed to cost asymptotically $a_{\mathrm{TF}}m^{7/3}$ when $m\to\infty$ and $Z/m\to\infty$. The question retains these same parameters and conventions. Within these assumptions, this is a claimed affirmative answer to the stated question.

\subsection{Formal verification}
For family 263, the documented entries give the large-$m$ ionization-energy coefficient and the generalized neutral outer-radius asymptotics. Fixed-$m$ limits and the general uniform excess-charge manuscript are not selected. The listed Comparator files are \texttt{CoulombIonization.lean}, \texttt{CoulombRadii.lean}. Named declarations include \texttt{OAI.CoulombAtom.generalized\_ionization}. This site has not executed the Lean code. The scope descriptions do not certify the remaining manuscript arguments.

\subsection{Status caveat}
OpenAI attributes these manuscripts to work by an internal model. Its release cautions that arguments without formalization can contain mistakes. This catalogue has not independently checked the proofs; the labels report the mathematical claims attributed to OpenAI.

